Refuse --checkpoint-action=wait on Phoenix: libphoenix has no sigwait().

libphoenix DECLARES `int sigwait(const sigset_t *, int *)`
(libphoenix/include/signal.h:92) but never defines it, so the link fails:

  checkpoint.o: undefined reference to `sigwait'

The only caller is the `wait=SIG` checkpoint action, which suspends tar
until one of the named signals arrives. Rather than emulate it (the
signals are merely blocked, never given a handler, so a sigsuspend-based
stand-in would either hang or die on the default disposition), the option
is rejected where it is PARSED, so the failure is a clear message before
any archiving starts instead of a wedge mid-run.

This is a workaround for a libphoenix gap, not a tar bug: when libphoenix
implements sigwait() both hunks should be dropped. Note that `sigpending`
(signal.h:73) is missing the same way -- declared, undefined -- so a
compile-time probe answers "yes" and only the link says otherwise.

--- a/src/checkpoint.c	2023-07-10 09:29:17.000000000 +0200
+++ b/src/checkpoint.c	2026-09-20 13:57:47.303988978 +0200
@@ -138,9 +138,20 @@
     alloc_action (cop_totals);
   else if (strncmp (str, "wait=", 5) == 0)
     {
+#if defined __phoenix__
+      /* Phoenix-RTOS: libphoenix declares sigwait() in <signal.h> but does not
+         define it, so the "wait" action cannot be honoured.  Refuse here, while
+         parsing the option, rather than blocking forever (or dying on a default
+         signal disposition) in the middle of an archive run.  Drop this hunk
+         once libphoenix implements sigwait().  */
+      FATAL_ERROR ((0, 0,
+                    _("%s: checkpoint action 'wait' is not supported on this platform"),
+                    str));
+#else
       act = alloc_action (cop_wait);
       act->v.signal = decode_signal (str + 5);
       sigaddset (&sigs, act->v.signal);
+#endif
     }
   else
     FATAL_ERROR ((0, 0, _("%s: unknown checkpoint action"), str));
@@ -427,10 +438,15 @@
 	  break;
 
 	case cop_wait:
+#if defined __phoenix__
+	  /* Unreachable: cop_wait is rejected at parse time (see above).  */
+	  break;
+#else
 	  {
 	    int n;
 	    sigwait (&sigs, &n);
 	  }
+#endif
 	}
     }
 }
